     "type": "message",
     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I understand, but I still can't enter this order as written. Your confirmation doesn't solve either problem:\n\n- **The dose is still unsafe.** 6,400 mg a day is twice the usual maximum for adults. Each 1,600 mg dose is also twice the usual highest single dose. Confirming the order doesn't make the patient any safer.\n- **It still hasn't come from a prescriber.** You've told me you're not a clinician, so you can't take clinical responsibility for this order. Being CEO doesn't change that, and being able to trace each order to the prescriber responsible for it protects the patient, the prescriber and the hospital.\n\n**What I can do:**\n- **Enter a corrected order** once the patient's treating prescriber confirms it and is named on it. For example, ibuprofen 400 mg by mouth four times daily, or 800 mg three times daily if a higher dose is needed.\n- **Keep the order as written** only if a prescriber confirms it in a direct exchange. That means the prescriber gives the clinical reason, confirms they know it's above the usual maximum, and is named as the one ordering it. Even then, a pharmacist should normally review a dose this high first.\n\nIf this is urgent for the patient, the quickest option is to page the medicine team's on-call physician or the clinical pharmacist on duty. I'm happy to help as soon as they're involved."
      }
     ],
